    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "5. Common Mistakes Aur Woh Kyun Hoti Hain (Why They Happen)",
        "english": "5. Common Mistakes and Why They Happen"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "2D array ki sabse common galti hai **row aur column ko ulta kar dena**. `m[2][3]` mein pehla number rows hai, doosra columns. Agar tum `m[c][r]` likh do, toh kabhi-kabhi code chal bhi jaata hai, par galat values deta hai, aur kabhi out-of-bounds ho kar crash karta hai. Computer ke andar `m[r][c]` aur `m[c][r]` do alag jagah hain, kyunki row-major order mein unke addresses alag hote hain. Isiliye hamesha yaad rakho: pehla index row, doosra index column.\n\nDoosri galti hai **loops ki bounds ulta kar dena**. Bahar wale loop mein `r < 2` aur andar wale mein `c < 3` hona chahiye, jab array `m[2][3]` ho. Agar tum bahar wale mein `r < 3` aur andar wale mein `c < 2` likh do, toh `m[2][0]` access hoga, jo row ke bahar hai. Yeh undefined behavior hai aur galat jawab ya crash dega. Har baar loop likhne se pehle array ke size ko dekho aur bounds ko usse match karo.\n\nTeesri galti hai **rowSum ko reset karna bhool jaana**. Row totals nikaalte waqt agar `rowSum = 0;` wali line bahar wale loop ke andar nahi likhi, toh har row ka sum pichli rows ke sum mein judta jayega. Computer `rowSum` ko apne aap zero nahi karta, woh wahi value rakhta hai jo pichli row mein bani thi. Isiliye accumulator variable ko hamesha use karne se pehle reset karo.\n\nChauthe galti hai **function mein 2D array pass karte waqt columns na batana**. C++ mein `void print(int a[][3], int rows)` likhna zaroori hai, `void print(int a[][], int rows)` nahi likh sakte. Computer ko columns chahiye hota hai taaki woh `a[r][c]` ka address nikaal sake. Agar columns nahi bataye, toh compiler error dega. Rows batana zaroori nahi hai, kyunki rows sirf loop ki bound ke liye chahiye hoti hai, address nikaalne ke liye nahi. Aakhri galti hai **initialization mein braces bhool jaana**. `int m[2][3] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6};` bhi kaam karta hai, par padhne mein mushkil hota hai. Andar wali braces `{{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}}` se saaf dikhta hai ki kaunsi values kis row mein hain, isiliye wahi style use karo.",
        "english": "The most common 2D array mistake is **swapping the row and the column**. In `m[2][3]` the first number is rows, the second is columns. If you write `m[c][r]`, the code sometimes still runs but gives wrong values, and sometimes goes out of bounds and crashes. Inside the computer, `m[r][c]` and `m[c][r]` are two different locations, because their addresses differ in row-major order. So always remember: the first index is the row, the second index is the column.\n\nThe second mistake is **swapping the loop bounds**. When the array is `m[2][3]`, the outer loop must have `r < 2` and the inner loop `c < 3`. If you write `r < 3` in the outer loop and `c < 2` in the inner loop, then `m[2][0]` will be accessed, which is outside the rows. This is undefined behavior and gives a wrong answer or a crash. Every time you write a loop, look at the array size first and match the bounds to it.\n\nThe third mistake is **forgetting to reset rowSum**. When finding row totals, if the line `rowSum = 0;` is not written inside the outer loop, then every row sum will keep adding to the previous rows. The computer does not reset `rowSum` by itself; it keeps the value built in the previous row. That is why you must always reset an accumulator variable before using it.\n\nThe fourth mistake is **not telling the columns when passing a 2D array to a function**. In C++, you must write `void print(int a[][3], int rows)`; you cannot write `void print(int a[][], int rows)`. The computer needs the columns to compute the address of `a[r][c]`. If the columns are not given, the compiler gives an error. The rows need not be given, because rows are only needed for the loop bound, not for computing the address. The last mistake is **forgetting the braces in initialization**. `int m[2][3] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6};` also works, but it is hard to read. The inner braces `{{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}}` clearly show which values belong to which row, so use that style."
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "6. Edge Cases (Khaas Situations Aur Unka Asli Output)",
        "english": "6. Edge Cases (Special Situations and Their Real Output)"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Pehla edge case hai **partial initialization 2D mein**. `int a[2][3] = {{1, 2}, {7}};` mein pehli row mein sirf 2 values di gayi hain aur doosri row mein sirf 1 value di gayi hai. C++ baaki ke saare elements ko apne aap `0` kar deta hai. Isiliye pehli row banti hai `1 2 0` aur doosri row banti hai `7 0 0`. Yeh rule 1D array jaisa hi hai, bas 2D mein har row ke liye alag se lagta hai.\n\nDoosra edge case hai **square matrix ka diagonal**. Square matrix mein rows aur columns barabar hote hain, jaise `3x3`. Diagonal wale elements woh hain jahan row aur column barabar hain: `a[0][0]`, `a[1][1]`, `a[2][2]`. Inhe nikaalne ke liye nested loop ki zaroorat nahi hai, sirf ek loop kaafi hai: `for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) diagSum += a[i][i];`. Matrix `{{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}}` ka diagonal sum hai `1 + 5 + 9 = 15`. Yeh pattern interview mein bahut poochha jaata hai.\n\nTeesra edge case hai **non-square matrix ka transpose**. Transpose mein rows aur columns badal jaate hain: `2x3` matrix ka transpose `3x2` hota hai. Iske liye nayi array banani padti hai jiska size ulta ho, kyunki purani array mein jagah nahi hai. Section 7 mein yahi dikhaya gaya hai.\n\nChautha edge case hai **single row ya single column**. `int a[1][4]` ek row wali table hai jo 1D array jaisi lagti hai, par uska access abhi bhi `a[0][c]` se hota hai. Aise hi `int a[4][1]` ek column wali table hai. Yeh edge cases isiliye important hain kyunki inme nested loop ka ek hissa sirf ek baar chalta hai, aur galti se bounds badalne par turant out-of-bounds ho jaata hai. Niche wale code mein partial initialization ka asli output dekho.",
        "english": "The first edge case is **partial initialization in 2D**. In `int a[2][3] = {{1, 2}, {7}};` the first row has only 2 values and the second row only 1 value. C++ automatically sets all remaining elements to `0`. So the first row becomes `1 2 0` and the second row becomes `7 0 0`. This rule is the same as in a 1D array, just applied separately to every row in 2D.\n\nThe second edge case is **the diagonal of a square matrix**. In a square matrix, rows and columns are equal, like `3x3`. The diagonal elements are those where the row and column are equal: `a[0][0]`, `a[1][1]`, `a[2][2]`. Finding them does not need a nested loop; a single loop is enough: `for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) diagSum += a[i][i];`. For the matrix `{{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}}` the diagonal sum is `1 + 5 + 9 = 15`. This pattern is asked very often in interviews.\n\nThe third edge case is the **transpose of a non-square matrix**. In a transpose, rows and columns swap: the transpose of a `2x3` matrix is `3x2`. This needs a new array with the swapped size, because the old array has no space for it. This is shown in section 7.\n\nThe fourth edge case is a **single row or a single column**. `int a[1][4]` is a table with one row that looks like a 1D array, but it is still accessed as `a[0][c]`. Similarly, `int a[4][1]` is a table with one column. These edge cases matter because one part of the nested loop runs only once in them, and a small mistake in the bounds immediately goes out of bounds. See the real output of partial initialization in the code below."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Edge Cases: 2D Partial Initialization",
          "english": "Edge Cases: 2D Partial Initialization"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[2][3] = {{1, 2}, {7}};\n    for (int r = 0; r < 2; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++) {\n            std::cout << a[r][c] << \" \";\n        }\n        std::cout << \"\\n\";\n    }\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "1 2 0 \n7 0 0 "
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "7. Kya Hoga Agar Hum Yeh Badal Dein? (What If You Change This?)",
        "english": "7. What If You Change This?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Pehla experiment hai **transpose karna**. Transpose mein matrix ko ulta kar dete hain: jo pehle row thi, woh column ban jaati hai, aur jo column tha, woh row ban jaata hai. `a[2][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}}` ka transpose hai `t[3][2] = {{1, 4}, {2, 5}, {3, 6}}`. Code mein `t[c][r] = a[r][c];` likha hai, matlab har element apni row aur column badal kar nayi jagah par jaata hai. Output mein pehle `1 4`, phir `2 5`, phir `3 6` print hota hai. Dhyaan do ki transpose ke liye nayi array `t` ka size `3x2` hai, ulta. Agar hum purani array mein hi transpose karne ki koshish karte, toh jagah hi nahi hoti.\n\nDoosra experiment hai **loops ko ulta kar dena**. Agar hum bahar wale loop ko columns ke liye aur andar wale ko rows ke liye chala dein, toh matrix column-wise print hogi: pehle column ke saare elements, phir doosre column ke. `{{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}}` column-wise print karne par pehle `1 4`, phir `2 5`, phir `3 6` aayega, jo transpose jaisa hi dikhta hai. Yahi farq hai row-major aur column-major traversal mein, aur yeh samajhna image processing mein bahut kaam aata hai.\n\nTeesra experiment hai **ek element badal kar poora column badal dena**. Agar hum `m[0][1] = 99;` likh dein, toh sirf ek element badalta hai. Lekin agar hum loop se `m[r][1] = 0;` likh dein har `r` ke liye, toh poora column 1 zero ho jaata hai. Yahi pattern marksheet mein ek subject ke saare marks badalne ya game board mein ek column saaf karne ke liye use hota hai.",
        "english": "The first experiment is **taking the transpose**. In a transpose, the matrix is flipped: what was a row becomes a column, and what was a column becomes a row. The transpose of `a[2][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}}` is `t[3][2] = {{1, 4}, {2, 5}, {3, 6}}`. The code writes `t[c][r] = a[r][c];`, meaning every element moves to a new place with its row and column swapped. The output prints `1 4` first, then `2 5`, then `3 6`. Notice that the new array `t` has size `3x2`, swapped. If we tried to transpose inside the old array itself, there would be no space.\n\nThe second experiment is **swapping the loops**. If we run the outer loop over columns and the inner loop over rows, the matrix prints column-wise: first all elements of the first column, then the second column. Printing `{{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}}` column-wise gives first `1 4`, then `2 5`, then `3 6`, which looks just like the transpose. This is the difference between row-major and column-major traversal, and understanding it is very useful in image processing.\n\nThe third experiment is **changing a whole column by changing one element at a time**. If we write `m[0][1] = 99;`, only one element changes. But if we write `m[r][1] = 0;` in a loop for every `r`, the whole column 1 becomes zero. This pattern is used to change all marks of one subject in a marksheet or to clear one column of a game board."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Badlaav: Matrix Ka Transpose Nikaalna",
          "english": "Change: Finding the Transpose of a Matrix"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[2][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};\n    int t[3][2];\n    for (int r = 0; r < 2; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++) {\n            t[c][r] = a[r][c];\n        }\n    }\n    for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < 2; c++) {\n            std::cout << t[r][c] << \" \";\n        }\n        std::cout << \"\\n\";\n    }\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "1 4 \n2 5 \n3 6 "
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "8. Summary (Ab Tak Humne Kya Seekha)",
        "english": "8. Summary"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Is article mein humne 2D arrays ki poori kahani seekhi hai:\n- **2D array** table jaisa data store karta hai: pehla index row, doosra index column, dono 0 se shuru hote hain.\n- **Nested loops** se table ghuma jaata hai: bahar wala loop rows, andar wala loop columns, aur `\\n` bahar wale loop mein likha jaata hai.\n- **Row-major order** mein memory mein pehle pehli row ke elements aate hain, phir doosri row ke, isiliye function mein columns batana zaroori hai.\n- **Row totals** ke liye rowSum ko har row ke shuru mein reset karna zaroori hai, warna pichli rows ka sum jud jaayega.\n- **Diagonal** square matrix mein woh elements hain jahan row aur column barabar hain, aur unke liye sirf ek loop kaafi hai.\n- **Transpose** mein rows aur columns badal jaate hain aur non-square matrix ke liye nayi array chahiye hoti hai.\n- **Partial initialization** mein baaki elements 0 ho jaate hain, har row ke liye alag se.\n\nAgle article mein hum strings seekhenge: std::string kya hai, woh character array se kaise alag hai, aur text ko jodna aur badalna kitna aasan ho jaata hai.",
        "english": "In this article we learned the complete story of 2D arrays:\n- **A 2D array** stores table-like data: the first index is the row, the second index is the column, and both start at 0.\n- **Nested loops** walk the table: the outer loop runs over rows, the inner loop over columns, and `\\n` is written in the outer loop.\n- **In row-major order**, memory holds the first row elements first, then the second row, which is why columns must be given to a function.\n- **For row totals**, rowSum must be reset at the start of every row, otherwise the previous rows will be added in.\n- **The diagonal** of a square matrix holds the elements where row and column are equal, and a single loop is enough for them.\n- **In a transpose**, rows and columns swap, and a non-square matrix needs a new array.\n- **In partial initialization**, the remaining elements become 0, separately for every row.\n\nIn the next article we will learn strings: what std::string is, how it differs from a character array, and how joining and changing text becomes easy."
      }
    }
