    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "4. Step-by-Step Dry Run (String Ke Characters Ko Trace Karo)",
        "english": "4. Step-by-Step Dry Run (Tracing the Characters of a String)"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Ab hum concatenation wale example ka dry run karte hain, har step mein string ki value aur uski length likh kar.\n\n- **Step 1:** `std::string s = \"Learn\";` chalta hai. `s` ki value hai `\"Learn\"`, length hai 5. Characters hain: `s[0] = L, s[1] = e, s[2] = a, s[3] = r, s[4] = n`.\n- **Step 2:** `s = s + \"2\";` chalta hai. Pehle `s + \"2\"` se nayi temporary string banti hai `\"Learn2\"`, phir woh `s` mein assign hoti hai. Ab `s` ki value hai `\"Learn2\"`, length hai 6. Naya character hai `s[5] = 2`.\n- **Step 3:** `s += \"Pro\";` chalta hai. Yeh `s = s + \"Pro\";` ke barabar hai, par seedha `s` ko badal deta hai. Ab `s` ki value hai `\"Learn2Pro\"`. Akshar gino: L(0), e(1), a(2), r(3), n(4), 2(5), P(6), r(7), o(8). Kul 9 characters, isiliye length hai 9. Hisson se bhi check karo: \"Learn\" mein 5, \"2\" mein 1, \"Pro\" mein 3, kul 5 + 1 + 3 = 9. Aakhri index hai 8, jo length - 1 hai.\n\nIs dry run se ek important seekh milti hai: length ginne mein galti aasani se ho jaati hai, isiliye hamesha hisson ko jod kar check karo aur code ko chala kar verify karo. Ab vowels wale loop ka chhota dry run dekhte hain. String hai `\"engineering\"` jiski length 11 hai. Loop variable `i` 0 se 10 tak jaata hai. Characters hain: e(0), n(1), g(2), i(3), n(4), e(5), e(6), r(7), i(8), n(9), g(10). Counter 0 se shuru hota hai. `i = 0` par `e` vowel hai, counter banta hai 1. `i = 3` par `i` vowel hai, counter banta hai 2. `i = 5` par `e` vowel hai, counter banta hai 3. `i = 6` par `e` vowel hai, counter banta hai 4. `i = 8` par `i` vowel hai, counter banta hai 5. Baaki saari positions par consonants hain aur counter nahi badalta. Final output hai `Vowels in engineering = 5`.",
        "english": "Now let us dry run the concatenation example, writing the value of the string and its length at every step.\n\n- **Step 1:** `std::string s = \"Learn\";` runs. The value of `s` is `\"Learn\"`, length is 5. The characters are: `s[0] = L, s[1] = e, s[2] = a, s[3] = r, s[4] = n`.\n- **Step 2:** `s = s + \"2\";` runs. First, `s + \"2\"` creates a new temporary string `\"Learn2\"`, then it is assigned to `s`. Now the value of `s` is `\"Learn2\"`, length is 6. The new character is `s[5] = 2`.\n- **Step 3:** `s += \"Pro\";` runs. This is equal to `s = s + \"Pro\";`, but it changes `s` directly. Now the value of `s` is `\"Learn2Pro\"`. Count the letters: L(1), e(2), a(3), r(4), n(5), 2(6), P(7), r(8), o(9). The length is 9. The indexes are 0 to 8, so `s[6] = P, s[7] = r, s[8] = o`.\n\nThis dry run teaches an important lesson: never trust a length you counted in your head without checking. Many students count \"Learn2Pro\" as 8 by mistake, forgetting one letter. The safe way is to count the parts: \"Learn\" has 5, \"2\" has 1, \"Pro\" has 3, and 5 + 1 + 3 = 9. The code block below shows the real output from the compiler, and the length there is 9.\n\nNow a short dry run of the vowel counting loop. The string is `\"engineering\"` with length 11. The loop variable `i` goes from 0 to 10. The characters are: e(0), n(1), g(2), i(3), n(4), e(5), e(6), r(7), i(8), n(9), g(10). The counter starts at 0. At `i = 0`, `e` is a vowel, so the counter becomes 1. At `i = 3`, `i` is a vowel, so the counter becomes 2. At `i = 5`, `e` is a vowel, so the counter becomes 3. At `i = 6`, `e` is a vowel, so the counter becomes 4. At `i = 8`, `i` is a vowel, so the counter becomes 5. All other positions hold consonants and do not change the counter. The final output is `Vowels in engineering = 5`."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Dry Run Example: Vowels Ginna Loop Se",
          "english": "Dry Run Example: Counting Vowels with a Loop"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string s = \"engineering\";\n    int vowels = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)s.length(); i++) {\n        char ch = s[i];\n        if (ch == 'a' || ch == 'e' || ch == 'i' || ch == 'o' || ch == 'u') {\n            vowels++;\n        }\n    }\n    std::cout << \"Vowels in engineering = \" << vowels << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "Vowels in engineering = 5"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "5. Common Mistakes Aur Woh Kyun Hoti Hain (Why They Happen)",
        "english": "5. Common Mistakes and Why They Happen"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "String ki sabse common galti hai **`#include <string>` bhool jaana**. Bahut se compilers `<iostream>` ke saath string ko bhi le aate hain, isiliye code kabhi-kabhi bina include ke bhi chal jaata hai. Lekin yeh guarantee nahi hai, aur doosre compiler par wahi code error dega: `string is not a member of std`. Isiliye hamesha `#include <string>` likho, chahe code bina uske chal raha ho.\n\nDoosri galti hai **single quotes aur double quotes ko mix kar dena**. C++ mein `\"A\"` ek string hai (characters ka group) aur `'A'` ek character hai (sirf ek akshar). Agar tum `std::string s = 'A';` likh do, toh compiler error dega, kyunki character ko string mein assign nahi kar sakte. Aise hi `s[0] == \"A\"` likhna galat hai, kyunki `s[0]` ek character hai aur `\"A\"` ek string hai. Compare karne ke liye `'A'` likhna padega. Computer ke andar `\"A\"` do bytes leta hai (A aur null terminator), jabki `'A'` sirf ek byte leta hai. Yeh farq samajhna bahut zaroori hai.\n\nTeesri galti hai **string ko `+` se number ke saath jodna**. `std::string s = \"Age: \" + 21;` nahi likh sakte, kyunki `+` string aur number ko jodna nahi jaanta. Computer yahan number ko string mein badalna nahi jaanta. Sahi tareeka hai `std::to_string(21)` use karna: `\"Age: \" + std::to_string(21)` se `\"Age: 21\"` banta hai. Ya phir `std::cout` mein chaining use karo: `std::cout << \"Age: \" << 21;`.\n\nChauthe galti hai **string ke bahar wala index access karna**. `s[s.length()]` string ke bahar hai, kyunki valid index hain 0 se length-1 tak. C++ mein `s[s.length()]` par null character milta hai, par use badalna ya usse aage jaana undefined behavior hai. Aakhri galti hai **`cin >>` se space wala text lena**. `cin >> name;` sirf pehla shabd leta hai, space ke baad ka hissa chhod deta hai. Agar naam \"Aarav Kumar\" hai, toh `cin >> name;` se sirf \"Aarav\" milega. Poori line lene ke liye `std::getline(cin, name);` use karna padta hai. Yeh farq aage ke input wale article mein detail mein aayega.",
        "english": "The most common string mistake is **forgetting `#include <string>`**. Many compilers also bring in string along with `<iostream>`, so the code sometimes runs even without the include. But this is not guaranteed, and the same code will give an error on another compiler: `string is not a member of std`. So always write `#include <string>`, even if the code runs without it.\n\nThe second mistake is **mixing single quotes and double quotes**. In C++, `\"A\"` is a string (a group of characters) and `'A'` is a character (just one letter). If you write `std::string s = 'A';`, the compiler gives an error, because a character cannot be assigned to a string. Similarly, writing `s[0] == \"A\"` is wrong, because `s[0]` is a character and `\"A\"` is a string. For the comparison you must write `'A'`. Inside the computer, `\"A\"` takes two bytes (A and the null terminator), while `'A'` takes only one byte. Understanding this difference is very important.\n\nThe third mistake is **joining a string with a number using `+`**. You cannot write `std::string s = \"Age: \" + 21;`, because `+` does not know how to join a string and a number. The computer does not know how to turn the number into a string here. The correct way is to use `std::to_string(21)`: `\"Age: \" + std::to_string(21)` makes `\"Age: 21\"`. Or use chaining with `std::cout`: `std::cout << \"Age: \" << 21;`.\n\nThe fourth mistake is **accessing an index outside the string**. `s[s.length()]` is outside the string, because the valid indexes are 0 to length-1. In C++, `s[s.length()]` gives the null character, but changing it or going beyond it is undefined behavior. The last mistake is **taking text with spaces using `cin >>`**. `cin >> name;` takes only the first word and leaves the part after the space. If the name is \"Aarav Kumar\", then `cin >> name;` gives only \"Aarav\". To take the whole line, you must use `std::getline(cin, name);`. This difference will be covered in detail in the input article."
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "6. Edge Cases (Khaas Situations Aur Unka Asli Output)",
        "english": "6. Edge Cases (Special Situations and Their Real Output)"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Pehla edge case hai **khaali string**. `std::string empty = \"\";` ki length hoti hai 0. Us par `.length()` 0 deta hai, `empty[0]` access karna galat hai, aur use print karne par kuch nahi chhapta. Khaali string ko check karne ke liye `empty.empty()` ya `empty.length() == 0` use karo. Yeh check bahut kaam aata hai, jaise form mein naam khaali toh nahi hai.\n\nDoosra edge case hai **comparison ka dictionary order**. C++ mein strings ko compare karte waqt characters ke ASCII values compare hote hain. Capital letters ki ASCII values chhoti hoti hain, isiliye `\"Hello\" < \"hello\"` true hai, kyunki `H` (72) `h` (104) se chhota hai. Aise hi `\"apple\" < \"banana\"` true hai, kyunki pehla character `a` (97) `b` (98) se chhota hai. Aur `\"app\" < \"apple\"` true hai, kyunki pehle 3 characters same hain aur chhoti string pehle aati hai. Yeh dictionary order wahi hai jo dictionary mein hota hai, bas capital aur small ka farq dhyaan rakhna hai.\n\nTeesra edge case hai **string ko index se badalna**. `std::string s = \"hello\";` mein `s[0] = 'H';` likh kar pehla character badla ja sakta hai. Lekin `s[5] = '!';` nahi likh sakte, kyunki index 5 string ke bahar hai. String ko bada karne ke liye `+=` ya `push_back` use karo, seedha bahar wale index par likh kar nahi.\n\nChautha edge case hai **string ki length ka type**. `s.length()` ek unsigned type `size_t` return karta hai. Agar tum `for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--)` likhoge, toh khaali string par `s.length() - 1` bahut bada number ban jaayega, kyunki unsigned mein -1 wrap ho jaata hai. Isiliye loop mein `(int)s.length()` likh kar pehle int mein badal lo. Niche wale code mein khaali string aur comparison ke edge cases ka asli output dekho.",
        "english": "The first edge case is an **empty string**. `std::string empty = \"\";` has length 0. Calling `.length()` on it gives 0, accessing `empty[0]` is wrong, and printing it shows nothing. To check for an empty string, use `empty.empty()` or `empty.length() == 0`. This check is very useful, for example to see whether a name field in a form is empty.\n\nThe second edge case is the **dictionary order of comparison**. When C++ compares strings, it compares the ASCII values of the characters. Capital letters have smaller ASCII values, so `\"Hello\" < \"hello\"` is true, because `H` (72) is smaller than `h` (104). Similarly, `\"apple\" < \"banana\"` is true, because the first character `a` (97) is smaller than `b` (98). And `\"app\" < \"apple\"` is true, because the first 3 characters are the same and the shorter string comes first. This dictionary order is the same as in a dictionary, except you must remember the difference between capital and small letters.\n\nThe third edge case is **changing a string by index**. In `std::string s = \"hello\";` you can change the first character by writing `s[0] = 'H';`. But you cannot write `s[5] = '!';`, because index 5 is outside the string. To grow a string, use `+=` or `push_back`, not by writing directly to an outside index.\n\nThe fourth edge case is the **type of the string length**. `s.length()` returns an unsigned type called `size_t`. If you write `for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--)`, then for an empty string `s.length() - 1` becomes a very large number, because -1 wraps around in unsigned arithmetic. That is why you should first convert it to int with `(int)s.length()` in the loop. See the real output of the empty string and comparison edge cases in the code below."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Edge Cases: Khaali String Aur Comparison",
          "english": "Edge Cases: Empty String and Comparison"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string empty = \"\";\n    std::cout << \"Empty length = \" << empty.length() << \"\\n\";\n    std::string a = \"apple\";\n    std::string b = \"banana\";\n    std::cout << \"apple < banana? \" << (a < b) << \"\\n\";\n    std::string word = \"C++\";\n    word = word + \" is fun\";\n    std::cout << word << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "Empty length = 0\napple < banana? 1\nC++ is fun"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "7. Kya Hoga Agar Hum Yeh Badal Dein? (What If You Change This?)",
        "english": "7. What If You Change This?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Pehla experiment hai **index se characters badalna**. Niche wale code mein `s = \"hello\"` hai. `s[0] = 'H';` se pehla character `h` se `H` ban jaata hai, aur `s[4] = '!';` se aakhri character `o` ki jagah `!` aa jaata hai. Ab string banti hai `\"Hell!\"`. Dhyaan do ki `s[4]` ko badalne se pehle string `\"hello\"` thi, jisme index 4 par `o` tha. Badalne ke baad woh `!` ho gaya. Yeh dikhata hai ki string ke characters ko array ki tarah badla ja sakta hai.\n\nDoosra experiment hai **string ko khud se jodna**. `std::string t = s + s;` se `t` banta hai `\"Hell!Hell!\"`, jiski length hai 10. Yeh dikhata hai ki `+` se string ko khud se bhi joda ja sakta hai, aur length double ho jaati hai. Agar hum `s += s;` likhte, toh `s` khud badal kar `\"Hell!Hell!\"` ho jaata.\n\nTeesra experiment hai **`+` ki jagah `+=` use karna**. `s = s + \"2\";` aur `s += \"2\";` dono ka result ek hi hai, par `+=` zyada efficient hai kyunki woh nayi temporary string banaye bina seedha `s` ko badal deta hai. Bade programs mein yeh farq performance mein dikhta hai. Aakhri experiment hai **comparison ka result badalna**. Agar hum `\"apple\" < \"banana\"` ki jagah `\"banana\" < \"apple\"` likh dein, toh result `1` ki jagah `0` ho jaayega, kyunki `b` `a` se bada hai. Comparison ka order badalne se jawab ulta ho jaata hai.",
        "english": "The first experiment is **changing characters by index**. In the code below, `s = \"hello\"`. `s[0] = 'H';` changes the first character from `h` to `H`, and `s[4] = '!';` replaces the last character `o` with `!`. The string now becomes `\"Hell!\"`. Notice that before changing `s[4]`, the string was `\"hello\"`, with `o` at index 4. After the change it is `!`. This shows that string characters can be changed just like an array.\n\nThe second experiment is **joining a string with itself**. `std::string t = s + s;` makes `t` become `\"Hell!Hell!\"`, with length 10. This shows that `+` can join a string with itself, and the length doubles. If we wrote `s += s;`, then `s` itself would change to `\"Hell!Hell!\"`.\n\nThe third experiment is **using `+=` instead of `+`**. `s = s + \"2\";` and `s += \"2\";` give the same result, but `+=` is more efficient because it changes `s` directly without creating a new temporary string. In large programs this difference shows up in performance. The last experiment is **changing the result of a comparison**. If we write `\"banana\" < \"apple\"` instead of `\"apple\" < \"banana\"`, the result becomes `0` instead of `1`, because `b` is greater than `a`. Changing the order of a comparison reverses the answer."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Badlaav: Index Se Characters Badalna Aur String Ko Khud Se Jodna",
          "english": "Change: Modifying Characters by Index and Joining a String with Itself"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string s = \"hello\";\n    s[0] = 'H';\n    s[4] = '!';\n    std::cout << s << \"\\n\";\n    std::string t = s + s;\n    std::cout << t << \" (\" << t.length() << \")\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "Hell!\nHell!Hell! (10)"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "8. Summary (Ab Tak Humne Kya Seekha)",
        "english": "8. Summary"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Is article mein humne std::string ki poori buniyaad seekhi hai:\n- **std::string** text handle karne ka modern tareeka hai, aur use karne ke liye `#include <string>` chahiye hota hai.\n- **Concatenation** `+` aur `+=` se hoti hai, aur `+` nayi string banata hai jabki `+=` purani ko badal deta hai.\n- **Length** `.length()` ya `.size()` se milti hai, aur index 0 se length-1 tak hote hain.\n- **Index access** `s[i]` se hota hai, aur characters ko array ki tarah padha aur badla ja sakta hai.\n- **Comparison** `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>` se dictionary order mein hoti hai, jisme capital letters small se pehle aate hain.\n- **Single quotes** character ke liye hain, **double quotes** string ke liye, inhe mix mat karo.\n- **`cin >>`** sirf pehla shabd leta hai, poori line ke liye `getline` chahiye hota hai.\n\nAgle article mein hum string ke powerful methods seekhenge: substr se hissa nikaalna, find se dhoondhna, aur compare se barabar check karna.",
        "english": "In this article we learned the complete foundation of std::string:\n- **std::string** is the modern way to handle text, and using it needs `#include <string>`.\n- **Concatenation** happens with `+` and `+=`, and `+` creates a new string while `+=` changes the old one.\n- **Length** comes from `.length()` or `.size()`, and indexes run from 0 to length-1.\n- **Index access** happens with `s[i]`, and characters can be read and changed like an array.\n- **Comparison** with `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>` follows dictionary order, where capital letters come before small ones.\n- **Single quotes** are for a character, **double quotes** are for a string; do not mix them.\n- **`cin >>`** takes only the first word; a full line needs `getline`.\n\nIn the next article we will learn the powerful methods of strings: extracting a part with substr, searching with find, and checking equality with compare."
      }
    }
