      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Is article mein humne reference ko andar tak samjha:\n- **Reference ek alias hai:** `int &ref = marks;` koi naya dabba nahi banata, woh `marks` wale dabbe ka doosra naam ban jata hai.\n- **Banate waqt binding zaroori hai:** `int &r;` compile error hai, aur ek baar judne ke baad reference ko dobara kisi aur variable se nahi joda ja sakta.\n- **Pass by reference:** Function ko asli variable milta hai, isiliye swap jaise kaam aur bade data ko bina copy ke bhejna possible hota hai.\n- **Range-based loop mein `&`:** `for (int &n : nums)` asli elements ko badalta hai, jabki `for (int n : nums)` sirf copies ko badalta hai.\n- **const reference:** `const int &` se tum function mein bina copy ke bade values bhej sakte ho aur seedha number bhi pass kar sakte ho.\n\nAgle article mein hum pointer arithmetic seekhenge — pointer ko aage-peeche badha kar array ke elements tak kaise pahuncha jata hai.",
        "english": "In this article, we understood references inside out:\n- **A reference is an alias:** `int &ref = marks;` creates no new box; it becomes a second name for the box of `marks`.\n- **Binding at creation is mandatory:** `int &r;` is a compile error, and once bound, a reference can never be re-bound to another variable.\n- **Pass by reference:** The function receives the original variable, which makes swap possible and lets large data be passed without copying.\n- **`&` in a range-based loop:** `for (int &n : nums)` changes the real elements, while `for (int n : nums)` only changes copies.\n- **const reference:** With `const int &` you can pass large values to a function without copying, and you can even pass a plain number directly.\n\nIn the next article, we will learn pointer arithmetic — how moving a pointer forward and backward reaches the elements of an array."
      }
