      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "7. Kya Hoga Agar Hum Yeh Badal Dein? (What If You Change This?)",
        "english": "7. What If You Change This?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Ab ek experiment karte hain: union mein daali gayi value badal kar dekho, aur enum ki starting value badal kar dekho. Neeche diye gaye code mein union ke `num` mein pehle `65` ki jagah `66` daala gaya hai, aur enum `Weekday` ki shuruaat `0` ki jagah `1` se ki gayi hai.\n\n- **Experiment 1: Union value badlo.** `d.num = 66;` karne par pehla byte `66` ban jata hai, aur `66` ka character `B` hota hai. Isiliye `d.ch` ab `B` dikhata hai. Ek number badla, character bhi badal gaya — kyunki dono ek hi bytes ko dekh rahe hain.\n- **Experiment 2: Enum ki starting value badlo.** `enum Weekday { MON = 1, TUE, WED, THU, FRI };` mein `MON` `1` hai, isiliye `TUE` `2`, `WED` `3`, `THU` `4` aur `FRI` `5` ban jate hain. Sirf pehli value badalne se poori series badal gayi.\n\nDono experiments ek hi baat sikhate hain: union mein value aur uska roop ek hi bytes se judte hain, aur enum mein ek value badalne se uske baad ki saari values apne aap badal jati hain.",
        "english": "Now let us run an experiment: change the value stored in the union, and change the starting value of the enum. In the code below, the union `num` is given `66` instead of `65`, and the enum `Weekday` starts from `1` instead of `0`.\n\n- **Experiment 1: Change the union value.** With `d.num = 66;`, the first byte becomes `66`, and the character of `66` is `B`. So `d.ch` now shows `B`. One number changed, and the character changed too — because both look at the same bytes.\n- **Experiment 2: Change the enum starting value.** In `enum Weekday { MON = 1, TUE, WED, THU, FRI };`, `MON` is `1`, so `TUE` becomes `2`, `WED` becomes `3`, `THU` becomes `4` and `FRI` becomes `5`. Changing only the first value shifted the whole series.\n\nBoth experiments teach the same lesson: in a union, a value and its form are tied to the same bytes, and in an enum, changing one value automatically shifts all the values after it."
      },
