        "hinglish": "Pehla example upar wale code block mein hai. Step-by-step samjho:\n\n- **Stack:** `push(10)`, `push(20)`, `push(30)` — stack ke andar `[10, 20, 30]` (30 upar). `top()` = 30. `pop()` ne 30 hataya, ab `top()` = 20. Dhyaan do: `pop()` kuch return nahi karta — pehle `top()` se dekho, phir `pop()` se hatao.\n- **Queue:** `push(10)`, `push(20)`, `push(30)` — queue ke andar `[10, 20, 30]` (10 aage). `front()` = 10, `back()` = 30. `pop()` ne 10 hataya, ab `front()` = 20.\n- **Deque:** `push_back(20)` se `[20]`, `push_front(10)` se `[10, 20]`, `push_back(30)` se `[10, 20, 30]`. Range-based for loop se deque ko seedha print kar sakte ho — stack aur queue mein aisa nahi kar sakte, kyunki unme iterator hi nahi hota!\n\n**Worked Example 2: Balanced Brackets — stack ka sabse famous use (neeche wale code block mein)** — yeh interview mein sabse zyada poochha jaane wala stack problem hai. String `\"{[()]}\"` ko check karna hai ki brackets balanced hain ya nahi. Tareeka: string ko left se right scan karo. Jab **opening** bracket (`(`, `[`, `{`) mile toh use stack mein push kar do. Jab **closing** bracket (`)`, `]`, `}`) mile toh stack ke top ko dekho — agar woh usi ka matching opening bracket hai toh pop kar do, warna string unbalanced hai. Aakhir mein agar stack khaali hai toh string balanced hai, warna kuch opening brackets band hi nahi hue. Neeche wale code mein `{[()]}` balanced hai (true = 1) aur `{[(])}` unbalanced hai (false = 0) — kyunki `]` aane par top par `(` tha, `[` nahi.",
