        "english": "The first example is in the code block above. Understand it step by step:\n\n- **Set:** `set<int> rollNumbers = {103, 101, 102, 101, 103};` — 5 values were inserted, but `101` and `103` appeared twice each. The set ignored the duplicates and sorted the rest. In the end only `{101, 102, 103}` remained, size = 3.\n- **Map:** `marks[\"Meera\"] = 92;` created the \"Meera\" key and set its value to 92. Then \"Aarav\" and \"Rohan\" arrived. The map kept the keys in sorted order: Aarav, Meera, Rohan — even though the insertion order was Meera, Aarav, Rohan. In the range-based for loop, `p.first` is the key and `p.second` is the value.\n- **`marks[\"Aarav\"]`** directly gave the value 85 — in O(log n) time, without scanning the whole map.\n\n**Worked Example 2: multiset with lower_bound/upper_bound (in the code block below)** — here `multiset<int> scores = {85, 92, 85, 70, 92, 85};`. `count(85)` = 3 (85 appears three times), `count(92)` = 2. Then `lower_bound(85)` returns an iterator pointing at the **first 85**, and `upper_bound(85)` returns an iterator pointing at the **first element after 85** (that is, 92). All the 85s lie in the range between the two — `distance(lower, upper)` gives the count: 3. This technique is very useful for **range queries**, such as \"how many marks lie between 85 and 92?\""
