        "hinglish": "### Worked Example 1 (set + map) ka dry run:\n- **Line `set<int> rollNumbers = {103, 101, 102, 101, 103};`:** Insert order: 103 aaya -> `{103}`. 101 aaya -> `{101, 103}` (sorted). 102 aaya -> `{101, 102, 103}`. 101 dobara aaya -> duplicate, ignore. 103 dobara aaya -> duplicate, ignore. Final set: `{101, 102, 103}`, size = 3.\n- **Print loop:** Set hamesha sorted order mein iterate hota hai: `101 102 103` print hota hai.\n- **Line `marks[\"Meera\"] = 92;`:** Map mein \"Meera\" key bani, value 92. Map: `{Meera: 92}`.\n- **Line `marks[\"Aarav\"] = 85;`:** Nayi key \"Aarav\" aayi — map ne keys ko sort karke rakha: `{Aarav: 85, Meera: 92}`.\n- **Line `marks[\"Rohan\"] = 78;`:** Map: `{Aarav: 85, Meera: 92, Rohan: 78}`.\n- **Print loop:** Sorted order mein print: `Aarav: 85`, `Meera: 92`, `Rohan: 78`.\n- **Line `marks[\"Aarav\"]`:** Key \"Aarav\" mili, value `85` print hui.\n\n---\n\n### Worked Example 2 (multiset + bounds) ka dry run:\n- **Line `multiset<int> scores = {85, 92, 85, 70, 92, 85};`:** Multiset sorted order mein store karta hai, duplicates ke saath: `{70, 85, 85, 85, 92, 92}`. Size = 6.\n- **Line `count(85)`:** Multiset mein 85 ko gin-ta hai: teen baar -> `3`. **Line `count(92)`:** Do baar -> `2`.\n- **Line `lower_bound(85)`:** Pehle 85 ki position — sorted list mein index 1. **Line `upper_bound(85)`:** 85 ke baad wala pehla element (92) — index 4.\n- **Line `distance(lower, upper)`:** Index 4 - index 1 = `3` — matlab 85 wale range mein 3 elements hain.\n- **Print:** `85 appears 3 times`, `92 appears 2 times`, `85s in range: 3` print hota hai.",
        "english": "### Dry run of Worked Example 1 (set + map):\n- **Line `set<int> rollNumbers = {103, 101, 102, 101, 103};`:** Insertion order: 103 arrives -> `{103}`. 101 arrives -> `{101, 103}` (sorted). 102 arrives -> `{101, 102, 103}`. 101 arrives again -> duplicate, ignored. 103 arrives again -> duplicate, ignored. Final set: `{101, 102, 103}`, size = 3.\n- **Print loop:** A set always iterates in sorted order: `101 102 103` is printed.\n- **Line `marks[\"Meera\"] = 92;`:** The \"Meera\" key is created in the map with value 92. Map: `{Meera: 92}`.\n- **Line `marks[\"Aarav\"] = 85;`:** A new key \"Aarav\" arrives — the map keeps keys sorted: `{Aarav: 85, Meera: 92}`.\n- **Line `marks[\"Rohan\"] = 78;`:** Map: `{Aarav: 85, Meera: 92, Rohan: 78}`.\n- **Print loop:** Prints in sorted order: `Aarav: 85`, `Meera: 92`, `Rohan: 78`.\n- **Line `marks[\"Aarav\"]`:** The key \"Aarav\" is found, value `85` is printed.\n\n---\n\n### Dry run of Worked Example 2 (multiset + bounds):\n- **Line `multiset<int> scores = {85, 92, 85, 70, 92, 85};`:** The multiset stores values in sorted order, with duplicates: `{70, 85, 85, 85, 92, 92}`. Size = 6.\n- **Line `count(85)`:** Counts 85 in the multiset: three times -> `3`. **Line `count(92)`:** Two times -> `2`.\n- **Line `lower_bound(85)`:** The position of the first 85 — index 1 in the sorted list. **Line `upper_bound(85)`:** The first element after 85 (92) — index 4.\n- **Line `distance(lower, upper)`:** Index 4 - index 1 = `3` — meaning there are 3 elements in the 85 range.\n- **Print:** `85 appears 3 times`, `92 appears 2 times`, `85s in range: 3` are printed."
