    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "7. Kya Hoga Agar Hum Yeh Badal Dein? (What If You Change This?)",
        "english": "7. What If You Change This?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Ab experiment karte hain: `map` ki jagah **`unordered_map`** use kar dein toh kya badalta hai? Neeche wale code mein wahi 3 students (Meera, Aarav, Rohan) dono containers mein daale gaye hain.\n\n- **Badlaav 1: Order badal gaya.** `map` mein output hamesha sorted order mein aata hai: Aarav, Meera, Rohan. `unordered_map` mein output ka order **unpredictable** hota hai — woh hash table par depend karta hai. Neeche wale output mein order hai Rohan, Aarav, Meera (tumhare compiler/run par yeh order alag bhi ho sakta hai!). Matlab agar tumhe sorted order chahiye toh `map` use karo; agar order se koi matlab nahi hai toh `unordered_map` fast hai.\n- **Badlaav 2: Speed badal gayi.** `map` mein find/insert/erase **O(log n)** hote hain (tree ki height). `unordered_map` mein yeh operations **average O(1)** hote hain (hashing ki wajah se). Bade data (lakhon entries) par unordered_map bahut fast hota hai.\n- **Badlaav 3: Kuch cheezein unordered_map mein nahi chalti.** `lower_bound`/`upper_bound` unordered_map mein hote hi nahi — kyunki wahan koi order hi nahi hai. Aur `unordered_map` mein iteration ka order har run mein badal sakta hai, isiliye us par bharosa mat karo.\n\nGolden rule: **sorted order chahiye toh `map`/`set`, sirf fast lookup chahiye toh `unordered_map`/`unordered_set`.** Dono ka interface lagbhag same hai — `insert`, `find`, `erase`, `count` dono mein chalte hain.",
        "english": "Now let us experiment: what changes if we use an **`unordered_map`** instead of a `map`? In the code below, the same 3 students (Meera, Aarav, Rohan) are put into both containers.\n\n- **Change 1: The order changed.** In a `map`, the output always comes in sorted order: Aarav, Meera, Rohan. In an `unordered_map`, the output order is **unpredictable** — it depends on the hash table. In the output below the order is Rohan, Aarav, Meera (on your compiler/run this order may differ!). So if you need sorted order, use a `map`; if order does not matter, an `unordered_map` is faster.\n- **Change 2: The speed changed.** In a `map`, find/insert/erase are **O(log n)** (the height of the tree). In an `unordered_map`, these operations are **average O(1)** (because of hashing). On large data (hundreds of thousands of entries), an unordered_map is much faster.\n- **Change 3: Some things do not work in an unordered_map.** `lower_bound`/`upper_bound` do not exist in an unordered_map at all — because there is no order there. And the iteration order of an unordered_map can change on every run, so never rely on it.\n\nThe golden rule: **if you need sorted order, use `map`/`set`; if you only need fast lookup, use `unordered_map`/`unordered_set`.** Their interfaces are almost the same — `insert`, `find`, `erase`, and `count` work in both."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Badlaav: map Ki Jagah unordered_map",
          "english": "Change: unordered_map Instead of map"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <map>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::map<std::string, int> ordered;\n    ordered[\"Meera\"] = 92;\n    ordered[\"Aarav\"] = 85;\n    ordered[\"Rohan\"] = 78;\n    std::cout << \"map (sorted by key): \";\n    for (const auto& p : ordered) std::cout << p.first << \" \";\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";\n\n    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> fast;\n    fast[\"Meera\"] = 92;\n    fast[\"Aarav\"] = 85;\n    fast[\"Rohan\"] = 78;\n    std::cout << \"unordered_map (no fixed order): \";\n    for (const auto& p : fast) std::cout << p.first << \" \";\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"unordered_map lookup Aarav: \" << fast[\"Aarav\"] << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "map (sorted by key): Aarav Meera Rohan \nunordered_map (no fixed order): Rohan Aarav Meera \nunordered_map lookup Aarav: 85"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "8. Summary (Ab Tak Humne Kya Seekha)",
        "english": "8. Summary"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Is article ke zaroori points:\n- **set:** Sirf values, unique + sorted. Duplicate insert ignore ho jaata hai. `insert`, `erase`, `find`, `count` (0 ya 1), `lower_bound`, `upper_bound`.\n- **map:** Key-value pairs, keys unique + sorted. `m[key]` entry bana deta hai (sirf padhne ke liye `at()` ya `find()` use karo). Iteration mein `p.first` key aur `p.second` value hoti hai.\n- **multiset:** Set jaisa par duplicates allowed. `count(x)` gin-ti deta hai. `erase(x)` saare copies hata deta hai — ek copy ke liye `erase(find(x))` likho.\n- **multimap:** Map jaisa par ek key ki kai values. `equal_range(key)` se ek key ki saari entries milti hain.\n- **Sab mein O(log n)** find/insert/erase hota hai (Red-Black Tree). Iteration hamesha sorted order mein hoti hai. Random access nahi hota — `s[2]` nahi likh sakte.\n- **Set ke elements const hote hain** — badalne ke liye pehle erase karo, phir insert karo.\n- **unordered_map/unordered_set** average O(1) lookup dete hain, par order unpredictable hota hai aur lower_bound/upper_bound nahi hote.\n\nAgle aur aakhri article mein hum iterators aur algorithms ki poori taqat seekhenge — sort, find, aur custom comparators ke saath.",
        "english": "Key takeaways from this article:\n- **set:** Only values, unique + sorted. A duplicate insert is ignored. `insert`, `erase`, `find`, `count` (0 or 1), `lower_bound`, `upper_bound`.\n- **map:** Key-value pairs, keys unique + sorted. `m[key]` creates an entry (for read-only access use `at()` or `find()`). When iterating, `p.first` is the key and `p.second` is the value.\n- **multiset:** Like a set but duplicates are allowed. `count(x)` gives the count. `erase(x)` removes all copies — for one copy write `erase(find(x))`.\n- **multimap:** Like a map but one key can have many values. `equal_range(key)` gives all entries for one key.\n- **All of them are O(log n)** for find/insert/erase (Red-Black Tree). Iteration is always in sorted order. There is no random access — you cannot write `s[2]`.\n- **A set's elements are const** — to change one, first erase it, then insert the new value.\n- **unordered_map/unordered_set** give average O(1) lookup, but the order is unpredictable and there is no lower_bound/upper_bound.\n\nIn the next and final article we will learn the full power of iterators and algorithms — sort, find, and custom comparators."
      }
    }
