      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Pehla example upar wale code block mein hai. Step-by-step samjho:\n\n- **Line `sort(..., [](int a, int b) { return a > b; })`:** Teesra argument ek **lambda comparator** hai. Sort jab bhi do elements `a` aur `b` ko compare karta hai, toh woh lambda ko call karta hai. Lambda `true` deta hai agar `a` ko `b` se pehle aana chahiye. `a > b` ka matlab hai \"bada wala pehle\" — isiliye result descending order mein aata hai: `95 92 92 88 78 71 65`.\n- **Line `find_if(..., [](int s) { return s > 90; })`:** `find_if` pehla aisa element dhoondhta hai jiske liye lambda `true` de. Sorted (descending) list mein pehla element hi `95` hai jo 90 se bada hai — isiliye `it` pehle element par point karta hai aur `*it` = 95 print hota hai.\n- **Line `count_if(..., [](int s) { return s >= 90; })`:** Poori range scan karke gin-ta hai kitne elements ke liye lambda true deta hai: 95, 92, 92 — kul `3`.\n\n**Worked Example 2: transform + reverse_iterator (neeche wale code block mein)** — yahan do naye concepts hain. `transform` har element par ek function chalata hai aur result ko doosri jagah likhta hai: yahan har marks ko 5 se badhaya gaya hai (grace marks). `rbegin()` aur `rend()` **reverse iterators** dete hain — `rbegin()` aakhri element par point karta hai aur `++` karne par peeche ki taraf badhta hai. Isiliye `for (auto it = v.rbegin(); it != v.rend(); it++)` se vector ulta print hota hai, bina vector ko badle.",
        "english": "The first example is in the code block above. Understand it step by step:\n\n- **Line `sort(..., [](int a, int b) { return a > b; })`:** The third argument is a **lambda comparator**. Whenever sort compares two elements `a` and `b`, it calls the lambda. The lambda returns `true` if `a` should come before `b`. `a > b` means \"the bigger one first\" — so the result comes in descending order: `95 92 92 88 78 71 65`.\n- **Line `find_if(..., [](int s) { return s > 90; })`:** `find_if` searches for the first element for which the lambda returns `true`. In the sorted (descending) list, the very first element is `95`, which is greater than 90 — so `it` points at the first element and `*it` = 95 is printed.\n- **Line `count_if(..., [](int s) { return s >= 90; })`:** It scans the whole range and counts how many elements make the lambda true: 95, 92, 92 — total `3`.\n\n**Worked Example 2: transform + reverse_iterator (in the code block below)** — here there are two new concepts. `transform` runs a function on every element and writes the result somewhere else: here every mark is increased by 5 (grace marks). `rbegin()` and `rend()` give **reverse iterators** — `rbegin()` points at the last element, and `++` moves it backward. So `for (auto it = v.rbegin(); it != v.rend(); it++)` prints the vector in reverse without changing the vector at all."
      }
