    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "7. Kya Hoga Agar Hum Yeh Badal Dein? (What If You Change This?)",
        "english": "7. What If You Change This?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Ab experiment karte hain: `sort` ka comparator badal kar **students ko naam se sort** karein, aur phir **marks se sort** karein — dono ka output compare karo. Neeche wale code mein `Student` struct hai jisme `name` aur `marks` hain.\n\n- **Badlaav 1: Naam se sort (`a.name < b.name`)** — comparator batata hai ki `a` ko `b` se pehle aana chahiye agar `a` ka naam alphabetically chhota hai. Output: Aarav (85), Meera (92), Rohan (78) — naam ke order mein, marks ke order mein nahi.\n- **Badlaav 2: Marks se sort (`a.marks > b.marks`)** — comparator badal diya toh output badal gaya: Meera (92), Aarav (85), Rohan (78) — ab sabse zyada marks wala pehle. Sort ka algorithm wahi hai, sirf comparison ka niyam badla hai. Yahi custom comparator ki taqat hai: **ek hi algorithm, alag-alag order**.\n- **Badlaav 3: Lambda ke andar capture `[&]`** — agar comparator ko bahar ka variable chahiye (jaise pass marks), toh lambda ke `[]` mein capture likhna padta hai. Neeche wale code mein `count_if` wala lambda `[passMarks]` capture karta hai — isiliye woh `passMarks` ko andar use kar sakta hai. Bina capture ke lambda bahar ke variables ko dekh hi nahi sakta.\n\nGolden rule: **algorithm ko mat badlo, comparator ko badlo.** Sort, find, count — yeh sab ek hi rehte hain; sirf lambda/comparator badal kar tum unse alag-alag kaam karwa sakte ho.",
        "english": "Now let us experiment: change `sort`'s comparator to sort **students by name**, and then **by marks** — and compare both outputs. The code below has a `Student` struct with `name` and `marks`.\n\n- **Change 1: Sort by name (`a.name < b.name`)** — the comparator says `a` should come before `b` if `a`'s name is alphabetically smaller. Output: Aarav (85), Meera (92), Rohan (78) — in name order, not marks order.\n- **Change 2: Sort by marks (`a.marks > b.marks`)** — changing the comparator changes the output: Meera (92), Aarav (85), Rohan (78) — now the highest marks come first. The sorting algorithm is the same; only the comparison rule changed. That is the power of a custom comparator: **one algorithm, different orders**.\n- **Change 3: Capture `[&]` inside a lambda** — if the comparator needs a variable from outside (such as the pass marks), the lambda's `[]` must capture it. In the code below, the `count_if` lambda captures `[passMarks]` — that is why it can use `passMarks` inside. Without a capture, a lambda cannot see outside variables at all.\n\nThe golden rule: **do not change the algorithm, change the comparator.** Sort, find, count — these all stay the same; by changing only the lambda/comparator you can make them do different jobs."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Badlaav: Ek Hi sort, Do Alag Comparators (Naam Vs Marks)",
          "english": "Change: One sort, Two Different Comparators (Name vs Marks)"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Student {\n    std::string name;\n    int marks;\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<Student> students = {\n        {\"Meera\", 92}, {\"Aarav\", 85}, {\"Rohan\", 78}\n    };\n\n    // Change 1: sort by name (alphabetical)\n    std::sort(students.begin(), students.end(),\n              [](const Student& a, const Student& b) {\n                  return a.name < b.name;\n              });\n    std::cout << \"Sorted by name: \";\n    for (const auto& s : students) std::cout << s.name << \" \";\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";\n\n    // Change 2: sort by marks (highest first)\n    std::sort(students.begin(), students.end(),\n              [](const Student& a, const Student& b) {\n                  return a.marks > b.marks;\n              });\n    std::cout << \"Sorted by marks: \";\n    for (const auto& s : students) std::cout << s.name << \"(\" << s.marks << \") \";\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";\n\n    // Change 3: lambda with capture [passMarks]\n    int passMarks = 80;\n    int passed = std::count_if(students.begin(), students.end(),\n                               [passMarks](const Student& s) {\n                                   return s.marks >= passMarks;\n                               });\n    std::cout << \"Students passed (>= 80): \" << passed << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "Sorted by name: Aarav Meera Rohan \nSorted by marks: Meera(92) Aarav(85) Rohan(78) \nStudents passed (>= 80): 2"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "8. Summary (Ab Tak Humne Kya Seekha)",
        "english": "8. Summary"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Is article ke zaroori points:\n- **Iterator** container ke element ki taraf point karta hai: `begin()` pehla element, `end()` aakhri ke baad wali position. `end()` ko kabhi dereference mat karo — pehle `it != v.end()` check karo.\n- **Iterator categories:** Random-access (vector, deque — `it + 5` chalta hai), bidirectional (list, set, map — sirf `++`/`--`), forward (forward_list, unordered_map). `sort()` ko random-access chahiye, isiliye woh list/set par nahi chalta.\n- **Algorithms `<algorithm>` mein hain:** `sort`, `find`, `find_if`, `count`, `count_if`, `reverse`, `transform`, `unique`, `binary_search`, `min_element`, `max_element`. `accumulate` `<numeric>` mein hai.\n- **Lambda** ek chhota anonymous function hai: `[](int a, int b) { return a > b; }`. Bahar ke variables use karne ke liye capture likho: `[passMarks]` ya `[&]`.\n- **Custom comparator** se ek hi algorithm alag-alag order de sakta hai — naam se sort ya marks se sort, sirf lambda badlo.\n- **Khatarnaak galtiyan:** `find` ka result bina check kiye dereference karna, erase ke baad purana iterator use karna (likho `it = v.erase(it)`), aur `unique()` ko akele use karna (hamesha `erase` ke saath aur sorted data par).\n- **Reverse iterators** (`rbegin()`/`rend()`) ulta iterate karte hain; **const iterators** (`cbegin()`/`cend()`) data ko badalne se rokte hain.\n\nBatch 5 yahin khatm hota hai: operator overloading se lekar STL ke iterators aur algorithms tak — ab tumhare paas C++ mein real programs likhne ke saare main tools hain. Agla kadam: in sab ko ek saath use karke apna khud ka project banao!",
        "english": "Key takeaways from this article:\n- An **iterator** points at a container's element: `begin()` is the first element, `end()` is the position after the last. Never dereference `end()` — check `it != v.end()` first.\n- **Iterator categories:** Random-access (vector, deque — `it + 5` works), bidirectional (list, set, map — only `++`/`--`), forward (forward_list, unordered_map). `sort()` needs random-access, which is why it does not run on a list or a set.\n- **Algorithms live in `<algorithm>`:** `sort`, `find`, `find_if`, `count`, `count_if`, `reverse`, `transform`, `unique`, `binary_search`, `min_element`, `max_element`. `accumulate` lives in `<numeric>`.\n- A **lambda** is a small anonymous function: `[](int a, int b) { return a > b; }`. To use outside variables, capture them: `[passMarks]` or `[&]`.\n- A **custom comparator** lets one algorithm give different orders — sort by name or by marks, just change the lambda.\n- **Dangerous mistakes:** dereferencing `find`'s result without checking, using an old iterator after erase (write `it = v.erase(it)`), and using `unique()` alone (always pair it with `erase`, on sorted data).\n- **Reverse iterators** (`rbegin()`/`rend()`) iterate backward; **const iterators** (`cbegin()`/`cend()`) prevent changing the data.\n\nBatch 5 ends here: from operator overloading to the STL's iterators and algorithms — you now have all the main tools to write real programs in C++. The next step: build your own project using all of them together!"
      }
    }
