      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "5. Edge Cases (Khaas Situations Aur Unka Asli Output)",
        "english": "5. Edge Cases (Special Situations and Their Real Output)"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Ab kuch tricky edge cases dekhte hain jo viva aur interviews mein bahut poochhe jaate hain:\n\n1. **Edge Case 1: Missing key par `[]` vs `at()` — bilkul opposite behaviour!**\n   `freq[\"zara\"]` likhne se key `\"zara\"` value 0 ke saath insert ho jaati hai aur size 1 badh jaata hai. Lekin `freq.at(\"zara\")` likhne se koi insert nahi hota — balki `std::out_of_range` exception throw hoti hai. Niche wale code mein pehle size 2 hai, phir `[]` se check karne ke baad size 3 ho jaata hai, aur phir `at()` ko try-catch mein pakda jaata hai.\n\n2. **Edge Case 2: `count()` hamesha 0 ya 1 hota hai.**\n   Kyunki keys unique hoti hain, `unordered_map` aur `unordered_set` mein `count(key)` kabhi 2 ya 3 nahi hota — sirf 0 (nahi hai) ya 1 (hai). Agar tumhe ek key kitni baar aayi hai yeh gin-na hai, toh value ko increment karo, `count()` ko nahi.\n\n3. **Edge Case 3: Khali container par `find()`**\n   Khali map par `find()` karna bilkul safe hai — woh `end()` return karta hai. Lekin `begin()` ko dereference karna (jaise `*m.begin()` khali map par) undefined behaviour hai.\n\n4. **Edge Case 4: Sab keys ek hi bucket mein (hash collision attack)**\n   Agar bahut saari keys ka hash ek hi bucket mein aa jaye, toh us bucket mein ek lambi linked list ban jaati hai aur search O(1) se O(n) ho jaata hai. Isiliye achha hash function zaroori hai. Real systems mein yeh ek security issue bhi hai — attacker jaan-boojh kar colliding keys bhej kar server ko slow kar sakta hai.",
        "english": "Now let us look at tricky edge cases that are frequently asked in viva and interviews:\n\n1. **Edge Case 1: `[]` vs `at()` on a missing key — completely opposite behaviour!**\n   Writing `freq[\"zara\"]` inserts the key `\"zara\"` with value 0 and grows the size by 1. But writing `freq.at(\"zara\")` inserts nothing — instead it throws a `std::out_of_range` exception. In the code below, the size starts at 2, becomes 3 after a `[]` check, and then `at()` is caught inside a try-catch block.\n\n2. **Edge Case 2: `count()` is always 0 or 1.**\n   Because keys are unique, `count(key)` on an `unordered_map` or `unordered_set` is never 2 or 3 — only 0 (absent) or 1 (present). If you need to count how many times a key appeared, increment the value; do not rely on `count()`.\n\n3. **Edge Case 3: `find()` on an empty container**\n   Calling `find()` on an empty map is perfectly safe — it returns `end()`. But dereferencing `begin()` on an empty map (like `*m.begin()`) is undefined behaviour.\n\n4. **Edge Case 4: All keys landing in one bucket (hash collisions)**\n   If many keys hash into the same bucket, that bucket grows a long linked list and search degrades from O(1) to O(n). That is why a good hash function matters. In real systems this is even a security issue — an attacker can deliberately send colliding keys to slow a server down."
      },
