    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "6. Kya Hoga Agar Hum Yeh Badal Dein? (What If You Change This?)",
        "english": "6. What If You Change This?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Marks file wale example mein ek badlaav karte hain: `ofstream` ko default mode ki jagah **append mode** mein khol kar marks file mein ek aur student jod dete hain, aur phir average dobara nikaalte hain.\n\nNiche wale code mein pehle hum `\"marks2.txt\"` mein 2 students likhte hain (Aarav 92, Meera 88). Phir file ko `ios::app` mode mein khol kar Rohan 75 jodte hain. Phir file ko padh kar average nikaalte hain: total = 92 + 88 + 75 = 255, count = 3, average = 85.\n\nAb socho agar humne doosri baar file ko default mode mein khola hota — toh Aarav aur Meera mit jaate, file mein sirf Rohan bachta, aur average 75 aata! Ek hi flag (`ios::app`) se poora result badal jaata hai. Yahi farq samajhna file handling ka sabse important lesson hai: **default mode = naya register, append mode = purane register mein aage likhna.**",
        "english": "Let us change one thing in the marks-file example: open the `ofstream` in **append mode** instead of the default mode, add one more student to the marks file, and then compute the average again.\n\nIn the code below we first write 2 students into `\"marks2.txt\"` (Aarav 92, Meera 88). Then we open the file in `ios::app` mode and add Rohan 75. Then we read the file and compute the average: total = 92 + 88 + 75 = 255, count = 3, average = 85.\n\nNow imagine we had opened the file the second time in the default mode — Aarav and Meera would have been deleted, only Rohan would remain in the file, and the average would be 75! A single flag (`ios::app`) changes the whole result. Understanding this difference is the most important lesson in file handling: **default mode = a new register, append mode = continuing in the old register.**"
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Badlaav: Default Mode Ki Jagah Append Mode — Average Badal Gaya",
          "english": "Change: Append Mode Instead of Default — Average Changed"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <fstream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::ofstream out1(\"marks2.txt\");\n    out1 << \"Aarav 92\\n\";\n    out1 << \"Meera 88\\n\";\n    out1.close();\n\n    // Badlaav: append mode se Rohan jodo (purana data safe)\n    std::ofstream out2(\"marks2.txt\", std::ios::app);\n    out2 << \"Rohan 75\\n\";\n    out2.close();\n\n    std::ifstream inFile(\"marks2.txt\");\n    std::string name;\n    int marks = 0, total = 0, count = 0;\n    while (inFile >> name >> marks) {\n        total += marks;\n        count++;\n    }\n    inFile.close();\n\n    std::cout << \"Total students: \" << count << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"Average: \" << (total / count) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "Total students: 3\nAverage: 85"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "7. Summary (Ab Tak Humne Kya Seekha)",
        "english": "7. Summary"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Is article mein humne file handling ki gehrai samjhi:\n- **ofstream** likhne ke liye, **ifstream** padhne ke liye, **fstream** dono ke liye. Sab `<fstream>` header mein hain aur `<<` / `>>` / `getline()` se kaam karte hain.\n- **Default ofstream truncate karta hai** — purana data mit jaata hai. Purana data bachana ho toh `std::ios::app` use karo.\n- **Hamesha check karo ki file khuli ya nahi** — `is_open()` ya `if (!file)` se. Bina check ke reads silently fail hoti hain.\n- **`>>` aur `getline()` mix karte waqt** beech mein bacha hua newline `ignore()` se nikalo, warna getline khaali line dega.\n- **Loop condition mein hi read karo** — `while (inFile >> name >> marks)` aur `while (getline(inFile, line))` khud hi end-of-file par ruk jaate hain.\n- **close() karo** — likhne ke baad file close karna data ko disk par flush karta hai.\n\nAgle article mein hum seekhenge ki C++ mein ek saath kai kaam (threads) kaise chalaye jaate hain — parallel programming ki pehli seedhi.",
        "english": "In this article we understood file handling in depth:\n- **ofstream** for writing, **ifstream** for reading, **fstream** for both. All are in the `<fstream>` header and work with `<<`, `>>` and `getline()`.\n- **A default ofstream truncates** — old data is deleted. To keep old data, use `std::ios::app`.\n- **Always check whether the file opened** — with `is_open()` or `if (!file)`. Without the check, reads fail silently.\n- **When mixing `>>` and `getline()`**, remove the leftover newline with `ignore()`, otherwise getline returns an empty line.\n- **Read inside the loop condition** — `while (inFile >> name >> marks)` and `while (getline(inFile, line))` stop by themselves at end-of-file.\n- **Call close()** — closing a file after writing flushes the data to disk.\n\nIn the next article we will learn how to run several jobs at once in C++ with threads — the first step of parallel programming."
      }
    }
