    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "4. Common Mistakes Aur Woh Kyun Hoti Hain (Why They Happen)",
        "english": "4. Common Mistakes and Why They Happen"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Preprocessor ke saath yeh 4 galtiyan sabse zyada hoti hain:\n\n1. **Macro mein brackets na lagana:**\n   - **Galti:** `#define SQUARE(x) x * x` likhna aur phir `SQUARE(2 + 3)` call karna.\n   - **Computer ke andar kya galat hota hai (WHY):** Preprocessor sirf text replace karta hai — use BODMAS ya operator precedence ki koi samajh nahi hoti. `SQUARE(2 + 3)` seedha `2 + 3 * 2 + 3` ban jaata hai, jo 11 hota hai, 25 nahi. Hamesha har argument aur poori expression ke aas-paas brackets lagao: `#define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))`.\n\n2. **#define ke aakhir mein semicolon lagana:**\n   - **Galti:** `#define MAX_MARKS 100;` likhna.\n   - **Computer ke andar kya galat hota hai (WHY):** Preprocessor `MAX_MARKS` ki jagah `100;` (semicolon ke saath) daal deta hai. Phir jab tum `int x = MAX_MARKS + 5;` likhte ho, toh woh `int x = 100; + 5;` ban jaata hai — ek extra semicolon aa jaata hai aur compiler error deta hai. `#define` lines par kabhi semicolon mat lagao.\n\n3. **Header guards bhoolna aur double inclusion:**\n   - **Galti:** Apni header file `student.h` mein guards na lagana, aur use do files se include karwana.\n   - **Computer ke andar kya galat hota hai (WHY):** Preprocessor header ka content dono baar copy kar deta hai. Usme likha class ya function do baar define ho jaata hai aur compiler bolta hai: `redefinition of class Student`. Fix: har header file ke shuru mein `#ifndef STUDENT_H`, phir `#define STUDENT_H`, aur aakhir mein `#endif` likho.\n\n4. **Macro argument ko do baar evaluate karwana (side effects):**\n   - **Galti:** `MAX(x++, y)` likhna.\n   - **Computer ke andar kya galat hota hai (WHY):** Macro expand hone par `x++` do jagah aa jaata hai: `((x++) > (y) ? (x++) : (y))`. Agar `x++ > y` true hua, toh `x++` dobara chalta hai — x ek ki jagah 2 badh jaata hai. Aur agar false hua, toh bhi ek baar badha hi. Yeh bug dhoondhna bahut mushkil hota hai. Fix: aise kaam ke liye macro ki jagah inline function ya template use karo.",
        "english": "These 4 mistakes happen most often with the preprocessor:\n\n1. **Forgetting brackets in a macro:**\n   - **The Mistake:** Writing `#define SQUARE(x) x * x` and then calling `SQUARE(2 + 3)`.\n   - **Why It Happens Internally:** The preprocessor only replaces text — it has no understanding of BODMAS or operator precedence. `SQUARE(2 + 3)` directly becomes `2 + 3 * 2 + 3`, which is 11, not 25. Always put brackets around every argument and the whole expression: `#define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))`.\n\n2. **Putting a semicolon at the end of a #define:**\n   - **The Mistake:** Writing `#define MAX_MARKS 100;`.\n   - **Why It Happens Internally:** The preprocessor replaces `MAX_MARKS` with `100;` (including the semicolon). Then when you write `int x = MAX_MARKS + 5;`, it becomes `int x = 100; + 5;` — an extra semicolon appears and the compiler reports an error. Never put a semicolon on a `#define` line.\n\n3. **Forgetting header guards and double inclusion:**\n   - **The Mistake:** Not putting guards in your header file `student.h`, and including it from two files.\n   - **Why It Happens Internally:** The preprocessor copies the header's content twice. Any class or function written in it gets defined twice and the compiler says: `redefinition of class Student`. Fix: at the start of every header file write `#ifndef STUDENT_H`, then `#define STUDENT_H`, and at the end write `#endif`.\n\n4. **Evaluating a macro argument twice (side effects):**\n   - **The Mistake:** Writing `MAX(x++, y)`.\n   - **Why It Happens Internally:** After macro expansion, `x++` appears in two places: `((x++) > (y) ? (x++) : (y))`. If `x++ > y` is true, `x++` runs again — x grows by 2 instead of 1. And even if it is false, it still grew once. This bug is very hard to find. Fix: for such jobs use an inline function or template instead of a macro."
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "5. Edge Cases (Khaas Situations Aur Unka Asli Output)",
        "english": "5. Edge Cases (Special Situations and Their Real Output)"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Ab preprocessor ke kuch edge cases dekhte hain:\n\n1. **Edge Case 1: Bina brackets wala SQUARE macro — galat jawab**\n   Niche wale code mein hum jaan-boojh kar galat macro likhte hain: `#define BAD_SQUARE(x) x * x`. Phir `BAD_SQUARE(2 + 3)` call karte hain. Preprocessor use `2 + 3 * 2 + 3` mein badal deta hai. BODMAS: pehle `3 * 2 = 6`, phir `2 + 6 + 3 = 11`. Output 11 aata hai, jabki sahi jawab 25 hai. Isi code mein sahi macro `GOOD_SQUARE` se 25 aata hai — dono ka farq ek hi output mein dikh jaata hai.\n\n2. **Edge Case 2: `__cplusplus` macro — kaunsa C++ version hai?**\n   Preprocessor ke paas kuch built-in macros hote hain. `__cplusplus` ka value batata hai ki compiler kis C++ standard par chal raha hai: C++17 ke liye yeh `201703` hota hai. Niche wale code mein hum ise print karte hain. Yeh interview mein poochha jaata hai.\n\n3. **Edge Case 3: `#pragma once` — header guards ka modern tareeka**\n   Aaj kal kai compilers `#pragma once` support karte hain — file ke shuru mein ek line likh do aur woh header sirf ek baar include hota hai. Yeh guards se chhota hai, lekin yeh standard C++ nahi hai — yeh ek compiler extension hai. Exams aur interviews mein guards wala tareeka hi likho.\n\n4. **Edge Case 4: Macro se string banana (`#` operator)**\n   Macro ke andar `#` likhne se argument ko string mein badal diya jaata hai. Jaise `#define STR(x) #x` — `STR(Hello)` `\"Hello\"` ban jaata hai. Yeh debugging macros mein use hota hai.",
        "english": "Now let us examine some edge cases with the preprocessor:\n\n1. **Edge Case 1: SQUARE macro without brackets — wrong answer**\n   In the code below we deliberately write a wrong macro: `#define BAD_SQUARE(x) x * x`. Then we call `BAD_SQUARE(2 + 3)`. The preprocessor turns it into `2 + 3 * 2 + 3`. By BODMAS: first `3 * 2 = 6`, then `2 + 6 + 3 = 11`. The output is 11, while the correct answer is 25. In the same code the correct macro `GOOD_SQUARE` gives 25 — the difference between the two shows in a single output.\n\n2. **Edge Case 2: The `__cplusplus` macro — which C++ version is this?**\n   The preprocessor has some built-in macros. `__cplusplus` tells you which C++ standard the compiler is using: for C++17 its value is `201703`. In the code below we print it. This is asked in interviews.\n\n3. **Edge Case 3: `#pragma once` — the modern way to do header guards**\n   Many compilers today support `#pragma once` — write one line at the start of the file and that header is included only once. It is shorter than guards, but it is not standard C++ — it is a compiler extension. In exams and interviews, write the guards approach.\n\n4. **Edge Case 4: Making a string from a macro (`#` operator)**\n   Writing `#` inside a macro turns the argument into a string. For example, `#define STR(x) #x` — `STR(Hello)` becomes `\"Hello\"`. This is used in debugging macros."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Edge Cases: Galat Macro 11 Deta Hai, Sahi Macro 25",
          "english": "Edge Cases: Wrong Macro Gives 11, Correct Macro Gives 25"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n\n#define BAD_SQUARE(x) x * x\n#define GOOD_SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << \"BAD_SQUARE(2 + 3) = \" << BAD_SQUARE(2 + 3) << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"GOOD_SQUARE(2 + 3) = \" << GOOD_SQUARE(2 + 3) << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"C++ version macro: \" << __cplusplus << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "BAD_SQUARE(2 + 3) = 11\nGOOD_SQUARE(2 + 3) = 25\nC++ version macro: 201703"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "6. Kya Hoga Agar Hum Yeh Badal Dein? (What If You Change This?)",
        "english": "6. What If You Change This?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Worked Example 2 mein `#define DEBUG_MODE` likha tha, jisse debug line print hoti thi. Ab badlaav karte hain: **DEBUG_MODE wali line ko hata dete hain** aur dekhte hain ki output kaise badalta hai.\n\nNiche wale code mein `#define DEBUG_MODE` line hai hi nahi. Preprocessor jab `#ifdef DEBUG_MODE` dekhta hai, toh use false maanta hai aur uske andar wali debug line ko compiler tak pahunchne hi nahi deta. Output mein sirf `Final Bill: Rs. 600` aata hai — debug line gayab.\n\nIsi code ko dobara `#define DEBUG_MODE` jod kar chalao, toh debug line wapas aa jayegi. Ek line se poora behaviour badal jaata hai — yahi conditional compilation ki taqat hai. Real projects mein aise flags se logging, testing aur alag-alag operating systems ke liye alag code likha jaata hai, bina code ko dobara likhe.",
        "english": "In Worked Example 2 we wrote `#define DEBUG_MODE`, which made the debug line print. Now let us change it: **remove the DEBUG_MODE line** and see how the output changes.\n\nIn the code below there is no `#define DEBUG_MODE` line at all. When the preprocessor sees `#ifdef DEBUG_MODE`, it treats it as false and never lets the debug line inside reach the compiler. The output contains only `Final Bill: Rs. 600` — the debug line is gone.\n\nRun the same code again after adding `#define DEBUG_MODE` back, and the debug line returns. One line changes the whole behaviour — that is the power of conditional compilation. In real projects, flags like this control logging, testing, and different code for different operating systems, without rewriting the code."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Badlaav: #define DEBUG_MODE Hataya — Debug Line Gayab",
          "english": "Change: #define DEBUG_MODE Removed — Debug Line Gone"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n\n// Badlaav: #define DEBUG_MODE line hata di gayi hai\n\nint main() {\n    int items = 5;\n    int price = 120;\n    int total = items * price;\n\n#ifdef DEBUG_MODE\n    std::cout << \"[DEBUG] items=\" << items << \", price=\" << price << \"\\n\";\n#endif\n\n    std::cout << \"Final Bill: Rs. \" << total << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "Final Bill: Rs. 600"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "7. Summary (Ab Tak Humne Kya Seekha)",
        "english": "7. Summary"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Is article mein humne preprocessor ki gehrai samjhi:\n- **Preprocessor compiler se pehle chalta hai** — `#` wali lines ko handle karta hai, comments hatata hai, aur code ko badal kar compiler ko deta hai.\n- **#include** header file ka content copy karke chipkata hai; **#define** macros banata hai jo sirf text replace karte hain.\n- **Macro mein brackets zaroori** — har argument aur poori expression ke aas-paas brackets lagao, warna `SQUARE(2+3)` 25 ki jagah 11 dega.\n- **#define par semicolon kabhi mat lagao** — warna replacement mein extra semicolon aa jaata hai.\n- **Conditional compilation** (`#ifdef` / `#ifndef` / `#endif`) se ek flag se code ko on/off kar sakte ho — debug mode iska sabse common use hai.\n- **Header guards** (`#ifndef FILE_H` / `#define FILE_H` / `#endif`) double inclusion aur redefinition error se bachate hain.\n- **Macro argument do baar evaluate ho sakta hai** — side effects (jaise `x++`) ke liye macro ki jagah inline function ya template use karo.\n\nAgle aur aakhri article mein hum poore C++ subject ka rapid-fire revision karenge — 10 core interview questions aur unke answers.",
        "english": "In this article we understood the preprocessor in depth:\n- The **preprocessor runs before the compiler** — it handles `#` lines, removes comments, and passes changed code to the compiler.\n- **#include** copies and pastes a header file's content; **#define** creates macros that only replace text.\n- **Brackets are essential in macros** — put brackets around every argument and the whole expression, otherwise `SQUARE(2+3)` gives 11 instead of 25.\n- **Never put a semicolon on a #define** — otherwise an extra semicolon appears in the replacement.\n- **Conditional compilation** (`#ifdef` / `#ifndef` / `#endif`) lets you switch code on or off with one flag — debug mode is its most common use.\n- **Header guards** (`#ifndef FILE_H` / `#define FILE_H` / `#endif`) prevent double inclusion and redefinition errors.\n- **A macro argument can be evaluated twice** — for side effects (like `x++`), use an inline function or template instead of a macro.\n\nIn the next and last article we will do a rapid-fire revision of the whole C++ subject — 10 core interview questions and their answers."
      }
    }
