    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "Q4. vector Vs list — Kab Kaunsa Use Karein?",
        "english": "Q4. vector vs list — When to Use Which?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "STL ke do sabse common containers hain `vector` aur `list`. Dono hi kai values store karte hain, lekin andar se bilkul alag bane hote hain, aur isiliye inki speed alag-alag kaam mein alag hoti hai.\n\n**vector** ek dynamic array hai — saare elements memory mein ek ke baad ek (contiguous) rakhe hote hain. Isiliye index se access O(1) mein hota hai (`v[100]` turant mil jaata hai), aur aakhir mein add karna (push_back) average O(1) hota hai. Lekin beech mein insert ya delete karna mehenga hai — O(n) — kyunki uske baad wale saare elements ko shift karna padta hai.\n\n**list** ek doubly linked list hai — har element alag jagah memory mein hota hai aur pointers se juda hota hai. Isiliye beech mein insert ya delete karna O(1) hota hai (bas pointers badalne hote hain), lekin index se access O(n) hota hai — 100th element tak pahunchne ke liye 100 nodes ko traverse karna padta hai.\n\nInterview ka golden answer: \"Random access aur end par add karna ho toh vector; beech mein bahut insert/delete ho toh list.\" Asliyat mein 90% cases mein vector hi jeet-ta hai, kyunki cache locality ki wajah se contiguous memory bahut fast hoti hai. Niche wale code mein vector ke aakhir mein push aur beech mein insert ka farq dikhaya gaya hai.",
        "english": "The two most common STL containers are `vector` and `list`. Both store many values, but they are built completely differently inside, so their speed differs for different jobs.\n\nA **vector** is a dynamic array — all elements sit one after another in memory (contiguous). So index access is O(1) (`v[100]` is found instantly), and adding at the end (push_back) is average O(1). But inserting or deleting in the middle is expensive — O(n) — because all elements after it must shift.\n\nA **list** is a doubly linked list — every element lives in a separate place in memory, connected by pointers. So inserting or deleting in the middle is O(1) (only pointers change), but index access is O(n) — reaching the 100th element means traversing 100 nodes.\n\nThe golden interview answer: \"For random access and adding at the end, use vector; for many inserts/deletes in the middle, use list.\" In reality, vector wins in 90% of cases, because contiguous memory is very fast thanks to cache locality. The code below shows pushing at the end of a vector and inserting in the middle."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Q4 Code: vector Mein Push_Back Aur Middle Insert",
          "english": "Q4 Code: push_back and Middle Insert in a vector"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 4, 5};\n    v.push_back(6);   // aakhir mein add: fast, O(1) average\n    std::cout << \"After push_back: \";\n    for (int n : v) std::cout << n << \" \";\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";\n\n    // Beech mein 3 insert karna: baad wale elements shift hote hain\n    v.insert(v.begin() + 2, 3);\n    std::cout << \"After insert at index 2: \";\n    for (int n : v) std::cout << n << \" \";\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"v[3] random access: \" << v[3] << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "After push_back: 1 2 4 5 6 \nAfter insert at index 2: 1 2 3 4 5 6 \nv[3] random access: 4"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "Q5. Copy Constructor Aur Rule of Three Kya Hai?",
        "english": "Q5. What Are the Copy Constructor and the Rule of Three?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Jab tum ek object ko doosre object se banate ho — jaise `Student s2 = s1;` — toh C++ **copy constructor** call karta hai. By default, compiler ek copy constructor bana deta hai jo **shallow copy** karta hai: har member ko ek-ek karke copy kar deta hai. Simple types (int, string) ke liye yeh theek hai, lekin jab class ke andar `new` se banaya hua pointer ho, toh shallow copy khatarnaak ho jaati hai.\n\nMaan lo class mein `int* data = new int[5]` hai. Shallow copy mein `s2.data` aur `s1.data` dono ek hi memory ki taraf point karne lagte hain. Jab `s1` destroy hota hai, toh uska destructor memory ko delete kar deta hai. Phir jab `s2` destroy hota hai, toh woh usi memory ko dobara delete karne ki koshish karta hai — **double free** — aur program crash ho jaata hai. Is problem ka solution hai **deep copy**: copy constructor mein nayi memory allocate karo aur values ko copy karo.\n\n**Rule of Three** kehta hai: agar tumhe in teeno mein se ek ko khud likhna pade — destructor, copy constructor, ya copy assignment operator — toh teeno ko likhna chahiye. Kyunki agar class mein raw pointer hai, toh teeno ki zaroorat padegi. Modern C++ (C++11) mein yeh **Rule of Five** ban gaya — move constructor aur move assignment operator bhi jud gaye. Lekin interview mein aksar Rule of Three hi poochha jaata hai.\n\nNiche wale code mein ek simple class hai jiska copy constructor deep copy karta hai: `s2` ki apni alag memory hoti hai. Jab hum `s2` ko badalte hain, toh `s1` par koi asar nahi padta — yahi deep copy ka proof hai.",
        "english": "When you create one object from another — like `Student s2 = s1;` — C++ calls the **copy constructor**. By default, the compiler generates a copy constructor that does a **shallow copy**: it copies every member one by one. That is fine for simple types (int, string), but when a class holds a pointer created with `new`, a shallow copy becomes dangerous.\n\nSuppose the class has `int* data = new int[5]`. With a shallow copy, `s2.data` and `s1.data` both point to the same memory. When `s1` is destroyed, its destructor deletes the memory. Then when `s2` is destroyed, it tries to delete the same memory again — a **double free** — and the program crashes. The solution is a **deep copy**: in the copy constructor, allocate new memory and copy the values.\n\nThe **Rule of Three** says: if you need to write one of these three yourself — the destructor, the copy constructor, or the copy assignment operator — you should write all three. Because if a class has a raw pointer, all three will be needed. In modern C++ (C++11) this became the **Rule of Five** — the move constructor and move assignment operator were added. But interviews usually ask about the Rule of Three.\n\nIn the code below, a simple class has a copy constructor that does a deep copy: `s2` gets its own separate memory. When we change `s2`, `s1` is not affected at all — that is the proof of a deep copy."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Q5 Code: Deep Copy — s2 Badlo, s1 Safe Rahe",
          "english": "Q5 Code: Deep Copy — Change s2, s1 Stays Safe"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n\nclass Marks {\n    int* data;\n    int size;\npublic:\n    Marks(int s) : size(s) {\n        data = new int[size];\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) data[i] = 0;\n    }\n    // Deep copy constructor: nayi memory, values copy\n    Marks(const Marks& other) : size(other.size) {\n        data = new int[size];\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) data[i] = other.data[i];\n    }\n    void set(int i, int v) { data[i] = v; }\n    int get(int i) { return data[i]; }\n    ~Marks() { delete[] data; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Marks m1(3);\n    m1.set(0, 90);\n    Marks m2 = m1;   // deep copy hoti hai\n    m2.set(0, 50);   // sirf m2 badla\n    std::cout << \"m1[0] = \" << m1.get(0) << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"m2[0] = \" << m2.get(0) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "m1[0] = 90\nm2[0] = 50"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "Q6. Smart Pointers: unique_ptr Aur shared_ptr Kab Use Karein?",
        "english": "Q6. Smart Pointers: When to Use unique_ptr and shared_ptr?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Raw pointer (`int* p = new int(5)`) ki sabse badi problem yeh hai ki tumhe khud `delete` yaad rakhna padta hai. Agar function ke beech mein exception aa jaye ya return ho jaye, toh `delete` kabhi nahi chalta aur memory leak ho jaata hai. **Smart pointers** is problem ka solution hain — yeh RAII par bane hote hain, matlab jab smart pointer ka scope khatam hota hai, toh uska destructor apne aap memory ko free kar deta hai.\n\n**unique_ptr** ka matlab hai \"sirf ek hi malik\". Ek unique_ptr ek object ka akela owner hota hai — use copy nahi kiya ja sakta, sirf move kiya ja sakta hai. Yeh sabse zyada use hota hai, kyunki zyadaatar cases mein ek object ka ek hi owner hota hai. `std::make_unique<int>(42)` se banta hai.\n\n**shared_ptr** ka matlab hai \"kai malik\". Kai shared_ptr ek hi object ko share kar sakte hain, aur andar ek reference count rakha hota hai. Jab aakhri shared_ptr destroy hota hai (count 0 ho jaata hai), tabhi memory free hoti hai. Yeh tab use karo jab object ka owner ek se zyada ho. Lekin dhyaan do: agar do shared_ptr ek doosre ko point karein, toh **circular reference** ban jaata hai aur memory kabhi free nahi hoti — iske liye `weak_ptr` hota hai.\n\nInterview ka golden answer: \"Default unique_ptr use karo; jab ownership share karni ho tab shared_ptr; circular reference todne ke liye weak_ptr.\" Niche wale code mein dekho: unique_ptr scope ke aakhir mein apne aap memory free karta hai — koi delete nahi likhna pada.",
        "english": "The biggest problem with a raw pointer (`int* p = new int(5)`) is that you must remember `delete` yourself. If an exception occurs or a return happens in the middle of a function, `delete` never runs and you get a memory leak. **Smart pointers** solve this problem — they are built on RAII, meaning when a smart pointer goes out of scope, its destructor frees the memory automatically.\n\nA **unique_ptr** means \"only one owner\". One unique_ptr is the sole owner of an object — it cannot be copied, only moved. This is the most commonly used one, because in most cases an object has exactly one owner. It is created with `std::make_unique<int>(42)`.\n\nA **shared_ptr** means \"many owners\". Several shared_ptrs can share one object, and a reference count is kept inside. Only when the last shared_ptr is destroyed (count becomes 0) is the memory freed. Use it when an object has more than one owner. But be careful: if two shared_ptrs point to each other, a **circular reference** is created and the memory is never freed — `weak_ptr` exists to break that.\n\nThe golden interview answer: \"Use unique_ptr by default; use shared_ptr when ownership must be shared; use weak_ptr to break circular references.\" See the code below: the unique_ptr frees its memory automatically at the end of the scope — no delete was written."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Q6 Code: unique_ptr — Bina delete Ke Auto Cleanup",
          "english": "Q6 Code: unique_ptr — Automatic Cleanup Without delete"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <memory>\n\nint main() {\n    std::unique_ptr<int> p1 = std::make_unique<int>(42);\n    std::cout << \"unique_ptr value: \" << *p1 << \"\\n\";\n\n    std::shared_ptr<int> s1 = std::make_shared<int>(100);\n    std::shared_ptr<int> s2 = s1;   // share: count 1 -> 2\n    std::cout << \"shared_ptr value: \" << *s1 << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"shared_ptr use_count: \" << s1.use_count() << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"Scope ends: memory auto-freed\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "unique_ptr value: 42\nshared_ptr value: 100\nshared_ptr use_count: 2\nScope ends: memory auto-freed"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "Q7. Templates Kya Hain Aur Compiler Unhe Kaise Handle Karta Hai?",
        "english": "Q7. What Are Templates and How Does the Compiler Handle Them?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Template ek blueprint hota hai jisse compiler alag-alag types ke liye asli code banata hai. Tum ek baar `template <typename T> T maxValue(T a, T b)` likhte ho, aur compiler `maxValue(10, 20)` ke liye int wala version aur `maxValue(3.5, 2.1)` ke liye double wala version bana deta hai. Is process ko **instantiation** kehte hain.\n\nInterview mein 3 baatein zaroor bolni hain. Pehli: **type deduction** — compiler arguments se `T` ko samajhta hai; mixed types (`maxValue(10, 3.5)`) par deduction fail hoti hai aur fix hai explicit type: `maxValue<double>(10, 3.5)`. Doosri: **template ka poora code header file mein hona chahiye** — kyunki compiler ko instantiate karne ke liye definition chahiye hoti hai; .cpp mein likhne se `undefined reference` linker error aata hai. Teesri: **STL templates par hi bana hai** — `vector<int>`, `sort`, `map` sab templates hain.\n\nEk aur interview favourite: \"Template aur function overloading mein kya farq hai?\" Jawab: overloading mein tum har type ke liye alag function likhte ho; template mein tum ek blueprint likhte ho aur compiler versions banata hai. Overloading compile-time par tumhare likhe functions mein se chun-ta hai; template compile-time par naye functions banata hai.\n\nNiche wale code mein ek hi template int aur string dono par chal raha hai — yahi templates ki taqat hai.",
        "english": "A template is a blueprint from which the compiler generates real code for different types. You write `template <typename T> T maxValue(T a, T b)` once, and the compiler creates the int version for `maxValue(10, 20)` and the double version for `maxValue(3.5, 2.1)`. This process is called **instantiation**.\n\nIn the interview, three points must be mentioned. First: **type deduction** — the compiler figures out `T` from the arguments; with mixed types (`maxValue(10, 3.5)`) deduction fails, and the fix is an explicit type: `maxValue<double>(10, 3.5)`. Second: **a template's full code must be in the header file** — because the compiler needs the definition to instantiate it; writing it in a .cpp file causes an `undefined reference` linker error. Third: **the STL is built on templates** — `vector<int>`, `sort`, and `map` are all templates.\n\nAnother interview favourite: \"What is the difference between a template and function overloading?\" Answer: in overloading you write a separate function for each type; in a template you write one blueprint and the compiler generates the versions. Overloading picks at compile time from functions you wrote; a template creates new functions at compile time.\n\nIn the code below, one template runs on both int and string — that is the power of templates."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Q7 Code: Ek Template, int Aur string Dono",
          "english": "Q7 Code: One Template, Both int and string"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nT maxValue(T a, T b) {\n    return (a > b) ? a : b;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << \"max int: \" << maxValue(10, 20) << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"max string: \" << maxValue(std::string(\"apple\"), std::string(\"mango\")) << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"max double (explicit): \" << maxValue<double>(10, 3.5) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "max int: 20\nmax string: mango\nmax double (explicit): 10"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "Q8. Exception Handling: try-catch Kab Aur Kaise Use Karein?",
        "english": "Q8. Exception Handling: When and How to Use try-catch?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Exception handling ka matlab hai: jab program chalte waqt kuch unexpected ho jaye, toh program crash hone ke bajaye us galti ko pakad kar gracefully handle kare. Teen keywords hain: **try** (risky code), **throw** (problem ka signal), **catch** (handling).\n\nInterview mein 4 baatein zaroor bolni hain. Pehli: **catch by const reference** — `catch (const std::exception& e)` — taaki object slicing na ho aur `what()` ka asli message mile. Doosri: **catch order** — specific catch pehle, `catch (...)` hamesha aakhir mein, kyunki catch blocks upar se niche check hote hain. Teesri: **stack unwinding** — exception throw hote hi control upar jaata hai aur raaste ke saare destructors chalte hain (RAII), isiliye cleanup guarantee rehta hai. Chauthi: **exceptions sirf exceptional galtiyon ke liye** — normal flow ki galtiyan (jaise galat input) if-else se handle karo, kyunki throw karna mehenga hota hai.\n\nEk aur common sawaal: \"Error codes vs exceptions — kaunsa behtar?\" Jawab: error codes mein har function ko return value check karni padti hai aur galti ignore ho sakti hai; exceptions mein galti ko ignore karna mushkil hai (catch na ho toh program terminate hota hai) aur error-handling code normal logic se alag rehta hai. Niche wale code mein ek hi try-catch do alag tarah ki galtiyon ko pakadta hai.",
        "english": "Exception handling means: when something unexpected happens while the program runs, instead of crashing, the program catches the error and handles it gracefully. There are three keywords: **try** (risky code), **throw** (the problem signal), and **catch** (the handling).\n\nIn the interview, four points must be mentioned. First: **catch by const reference** — `catch (const std::exception& e)` — so no object slicing happens and `what()` gives the real message. Second: **catch order** — specific catches first, `catch (...)` always last, because catch blocks are checked from top to bottom. Third: **stack unwinding** — the moment an exception is thrown, control moves upward and all destructors on the way run (RAII), so cleanup is guaranteed. Fourth: **exceptions are only for exceptional errors** — handle normal-flow mistakes (like wrong input) with if-else, because throwing is expensive.\n\nAnother common question: \"Error codes vs exceptions — which is better?\" Answer: with error codes every function must check the return value, and an error can be silently ignored; with exceptions an error is hard to ignore (without a catch the program terminates) and the error-handling code stays separate from the normal logic. In the code below, one try-catch handles two different kinds of errors."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Q8 Code: Ek try-catch, Do Alag Exceptions",
          "english": "Q8 Code: One try-catch, Two Different Exceptions"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <stdexcept>\n\ndouble safeDivide(double a, double b) {\n    if (b == 0) throw std::runtime_error(\"Division by zero\");\n    return a / b;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    try {\n        std::cout << \"10 / 2 = \" << safeDivide(10, 2) << \"\\n\";\n        std::cout << \"10 / 0 = \" << safeDivide(10, 0) << \"\\n\";\n    } catch (const std::runtime_error& e) {\n        std::cout << \"Caught: \" << e.what() << \"\\n\";\n    }\n    std::cout << \"Program still running\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "10 / 2 = 5\n10 / 0 = Caught: Division by zero\nProgram still running"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "Q9. static Keyword Ke 3 Matlab Kya Hain?",
        "english": "Q9. What Are the 3 Meanings of the static Keyword?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "`static` ek aisa keyword hai jiska matlab context ke hisaab se badal jaata hai — isiliye interview mein yeh bahut poochha jaata hai. Iske 3 main uses hain.\n\n**Pehla: static local variable.** Function ke andar `static int count = 0;` likhne par woh variable sirf ek baar banta hai aur function ke calls ke beech mein apni value bachaye rakhta hai. Normal local variable har call par dobara banta hai aur khatam ho jaata hai, lekin static variable poore program ke liye ek hi baar banta hai.\n\n**Doosra: static member variable.** Class ke andar `static int totalStudents;` likhne par woh variable class ke saare objects ke beech mein share hota hai — ek hi copy hoti hai, chahe 100 objects banein. Isse class ke bahar define bhi karna padta hai: `int Student::totalStudents = 0;`.\n\n**Teesra: static member function.** `static` function ko object ke bina bhi call kar sakte ho — `Student::showTotal()` — lekin woh sirf static members ko access kar sakta hai, kyunki uske paas `this` pointer nahi hota.\n\nNiche wale code mein `callCount` static local variable hai: pehli call par 1, doosri par 2, teesri par 3 print hota hai — value calls ke beech mein bachi rehti hai. Yahi static ka kamaal hai.",
        "english": "The `static` keyword is one whose meaning changes with context — which is why it is asked so often in interviews. It has 3 main uses.\n\n**First: a static local variable.** Writing `static int count = 0;` inside a function means the variable is created only once and keeps its value between calls. A normal local variable is created fresh on every call and destroyed, but a static variable is created once for the whole program.\n\n**Second: a static member variable.** Writing `static int totalStudents;` inside a class means the variable is shared among all objects of the class — there is only one copy, even if 100 objects are created. It must also be defined outside the class: `int Student::totalStudents = 0;`.\n\n**Third: a static member function.** A `static` function can be called without an object — `Student::showTotal()` — but it can only access static members, because it has no `this` pointer.\n\nIn the code below, `callCount` is a static local variable: the first call prints 1, the second prints 2, the third prints 3 — the value survives between calls. That is the power of static."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Q9 Code: static Local Variable Value Bachata Hai",
          "english": "Q9 Code: A static Local Variable Keeps Its Value"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n\nvoid visitCounter() {\n    static int count = 0;   // sirf ek baar banta hai\n    count++;\n    std::cout << \"Visit number: \" << count << \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    visitCounter();\n    visitCounter();\n    visitCounter();\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "Visit number: 1\nVisit number: 2\nVisit number: 3"
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "hinglish": "Q10. const, constexpr Aur mutable — Kab Kaunsa?",
        "english": "Q10. const, constexpr and mutable — When to Use Which?"
      },
      "body": {
        "hinglish": "Yeh teeno keywords \"badlaav\" ko control karte hain, aur interview mein inke farq par sawaal aana pakka hai.\n\n**const** ka matlab hai \"yeh value badlegi nahi\". `const int MAX = 100;` ke baad MAX ko badla nahi ja sakta. Function mein `void show() const` likhne ka matlab hai ki yeh function object ke members ko badlega nahi. `const` reference parameter (`const std::string& s`) se badi objects ko bina copy ke safe tareeke se pass kiya jaata hai.\n\n**constexpr** ka matlab hai \"yeh value compile time par hi nikaali ja sakti hai\". `constexpr int square(int x) { return x * x; }` ko compiler compile time par hi solve kar deta hai, jab argument constant ho. `constexpr` `const` se zyada strong hai — har constexpr const hota hai, lekin har const constexpr nahi hota.\n\n**mutable** ek khaas exception hai: `mutable` member ko `const` function ke andar bhi badla ja sakta hai. Yeh tab use hota hai jab koi member logically object ka hissa nahi hai — jaise ek cache ya ek counter jo sirf performance ke liye hai. Interview mein example do: ek class mein `mutable int accessCount;` jo `const` function mein bhi badh sakta hai.\n\nNiche wale code mein `constexpr` se compile-time calculation aur `const` function ka example hai — dono ka jawab ek hi output mein dikh raha hai.",
        "english": "These three keywords control \"change\", and interview questions about their differences are guaranteed.\n\n**const** means \"this value will not change\". After `const int MAX = 100;`, MAX cannot be changed. Writing `void show() const` in a function means the function will not change the object's members. A `const` reference parameter (`const std::string& s`) passes large objects safely without copying.\n\n**constexpr** means \"this value can be computed at compile time\". `constexpr int square(int x) { return x * x; }` is solved by the compiler at compile time when the argument is constant. `constexpr` is stronger than `const` — every constexpr is const, but not every const is constexpr.\n\n**mutable** is a special exception: a `mutable` member can be changed even inside a `const` function. It is used when a member is not logically part of the object — like a cache or a counter kept only for performance. Give this example in the interview: a class with `mutable int accessCount;` that can grow even inside a `const` function.\n\nIn the code below there is a compile-time calculation with `constexpr` and an example of a `const` function — both answers show in one output."
      },
      "code": {
        "title": {
          "hinglish": "Q10 Code: constexpr Compile-Time Aur const Function",
          "english": "Q10 Code: constexpr at Compile Time and a const Function"
        },
        "language": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <iostream>\n\nconstexpr int square(int x) {\n    return x * x;\n}\n\nclass Circle {\n    double radius;\npublic:\n    Circle(double r) : radius(r) {}\n    double area() const {   // const function: members nahi badalta\n        return 3.14159 * radius * radius;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    constexpr int s = square(7);   // compile time par solve hota hai\n    std::cout << \"square(7) = \" << s << \"\\n\";\n    Circle c(5.0);\n    std::cout << \"Circle area: \" << c.area() << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
        "output": "square(7) = 49\nCircle area: 78.5397"
      }
    }
